a conditional's type is not whichever arm parsed last.

MEASURED, and the two lines differ only in the ORDER OF THE ARMS:

    struct S { int i; };
    (c ? 0 : s)->i      accepted
    (c ? s : 0)->i      ERROR in lookup_member int->i does not exist

Both arms of a ternary call expression(), each of which leaves its own type in
current_target, and nothing put them back together. So the type of the whole
conditional was whatever the FALSE arm happened to leave -- which is right by
accident when the pointer is written second and wrong when it is written
first.

WHERE IT BITES. tcc writes all four orders on consecutive lines, in the two
files that gate the most:

    tests/tests2/33_ternary_op.c:37     int i1 = (i != 0 ? 0 : s)->i;
                                :38     int i2 = (i == 0 ? s : 0)->i;
                                :39     int i3 = (i != 0 ? (void*)0 : s)->i;
                                :40     int i4 = (i == 0 ? s : (void*)0)->i;
    tests/tcctest.c:4080-4083           the same four

tcctest.c is the file test1, test2 and test3 all begin with, so this is the
gate on tcc's own test suite. The gcc-built control passes both files from the
identical source, which is what says the fault is here and not in tcc.

C 6.5.15p6: when one arm is a NULL POINTER CONSTANT, the type of the
conditional is the OTHER arm's. micro-c cannot see whether an expression is
constant, and it does not need to -- a null pointer constant has exactly two
spellings and each is decided by what it leaves in current_target:

    integer 0    promote_type ALREADY decides this correctly. Its rule is that
                 the wider type wins, and a pointer is wider than an int on
                 every architecture here. The ternary simply never called it.

    (void*)0     ties with a struct pointer on width, so promote_type falls
                 through to its signedness tie-break and returns whichever
                 came first. Compared by IDENTITY against the type object
                 initialize_types built -- not by name, so a user typedef
                 called "void*" cannot collide with it.

WHAT THIS IS PERMISSIVE ABOUT, SAID PLAINLY. A conditional between a genuine
void* VALUE and an object pointer has type void* in C, and gets the object
pointer's type here; there is no constant information to tell that apart from
(void*)0. The effect is that micro-c ACCEPTS `(c ? vp : s)->i`, which C
rejects. It cannot miscompile anything -- both are eight-byte addresses, and
the type only decides which member offsets apply, which C would not let you
ask for at all. Accepting slightly too much is the right direction for a
bootstrap compiler; rejecting valid C is what stopped tcc.

MEASURED AFTER, locally, amd64 native:

    difftest    96 pass, 0 fail, 1 known gap    unchanged
    corpus      419 of 426                      unchanged

Case 113 covers it. A SECOND and INDEPENDENT defect sits directly behind this
one and is NOT fixed here -- dereferencing the result of a conditional
segfaults, minimally

    int v = *(1 ? p1 : p2);

with plain int pointers and no struct in sight. It reproduces identically
under the UNPATCHED compiler, so it predates this change; it was simply
unreachable while the type error stopped the parse first. Case 116 carries it
as a declared KNOWN GAP, with the reduction.

